                  Isopure Zero Carb is our top pick because it packs about 25 g of protein into roughly 100 calories with almost no fat, per the label, and mixes thin. Unjury Unflavored is best for stirring into food, and clear whey is often easiest on queasy days. Ask your prescriber or dietitian about your target first.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>How much protein should I eat on semaglutide or tirzepatide?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  A 2025 joint advisory from four US nutrition and obesity societies suggests targets based on adjusted body weight, about 1.5 g per kilogram of fat-free mass, or an absolute target of roughly 80 to 120 g a day. Your needs depend on your size and health, so agree a number with your prescriber or a dietitian.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Do GLP-1 medications cause muscle loss?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Some of the weight lost on any large weight loss is lean mass. In the STEP 1 semaglutide trial, a DXA substudy found about 38 percent of weight lost was lean mass, which includes water and organs as well as muscle. Adequate protein and regular strength training are the main ways to limit it.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Can protein shakes help with GLP-1 nausea?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  They can make it easier to keep protein up while nauseous, but they do not treat nausea. Very cold, thin drinks such as clear whey, sipped slowly, or small savory options are often easier than creamy shakes. Persistent vomiting, inability to keep fluids down or severe pain need prompt contact with your prescriber.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Is whey or plant protein better on a GLP-1?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Whey isolate is usually the most practical, with high protein per calorie, very little fat and lactose, and a thin texture. A single-ingredient pea isolate is a good dairy-free alternative, though it is thicker and earthier. Choose whichever your stomach tolerates best, and avoid sugar alcohols in either.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Should I drink protein shakes instead of meals on a GLP-1?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Generally no, unless your clinician has prescribed a structured meal replacement plan. Whole foods supply vitamins, minerals and fiber that shakes lack, and reduced intake already raises the risk of deficiencies. Most people do best with protein-focused small meals plus one or two shakes to close the gap.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Is it safe to take protein powder with Ozempic, Wegovy or Zepbound?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  There is no known interaction between protein powder and these medications, and nutrition societies encourage adequate protein during treatment. People with kidney disease, diabetes on other medicines, pancreatitis history or pregnancy should check with their doctor first, and anyone with severe side effects should contact their prescriber.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

